  "slug": "bifurcation-topic-8fba",
  "summary": "Three recent preprints model how spatial coupling between neurons, glia, vasculature and layers produces self-organized and patterned network states.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is that spatially distributed coupling between cellular and population compartments determines emergent circuit regimes. A two-dimensional neuron-glial model with Hodgkin-Huxley neurons, astrocytic potassium buffering and glutamate spillover reports self-organized fractional silencing of pyramidal neurons under global depolarization. A spatial neurogliovascular unit model shows that arachidonic acid metabolite effects can control the stability of cortical spreading depression waves. A two-layer neural field with differential interlayer coupling produces a Turing instability, offered as a theoretical mechanism relevant to Parkinson's disease and deep brain stimulation. What is new across these preprints is the emphasis on non-neuronal coupling, glial buffering and vascular metabolite signalling as architectural determinants alongside interlayer coupling. The models differ in class, so the link is architectural rather than methodological.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
